.TH TIMESHIFT-TRAY 1 "2026-09-01" "timeshift-tray" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timeshift\-tray \- Timeshift snapshot status in the system tray
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timeshift\-tray
.RI [ OPTION ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timeshift\-tray
puts a status icon in the desktop's tray showing when the last snapshot was
taken, whether scheduled snapshots are actually running, whether the snapshot
location is reachable, and live progress for whatever snapshot the machine is
taking \- including one started automatically before an apt operation, which is
otherwise invisible.
.PP
It runs as your own desktop user and never as root. It reads the
.B read\-only
part of the Timeshift daemon's interface over
.IR /run/timeshift/daemon.sock ,
which cannot create, delete or restore anything. Taking a snapshot is a
separate, authenticated action: a fixed\-argument wrapper is run through
.BR pkexec (1)
under a policy that authorises exactly that one command.
.PP
The applet is started automatically at login by
.IR /etc/xdg/autostart/timeshift\-tray.desktop .
After using the menu's Quit it can be started again from the desktop's
application list, or by running this command.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log what the applet is doing to standard error. Under autostart that reaches
the session journal
.RB ( "journalctl \-\-user \-b" ).
The environment variable
.B TIMESHIFT_TRAY_DEBUG=1
does the same without editing the autostart entry, which is a conffile.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TIMESHIFT_TRAY_DEBUG=1
The same as
.BR \-\-debug .
.TP
.B TIMESHIFT_TRAY_ICONS=auto|symbolic|colour
How the panel icon is drawn. The icon is Timeshift's shield, with the state
cut out of it: a tick, a ring while a snapshot runs, a triangle or round badge
for warning and error, and an outline when the status cannot be read. With
.B auto
(the default) it is monochrome, recoloured by the panel like the desktop's own
indicators, until something needs attention \- then the amber or red brand
shield appears, so colour on the panel always means something.
.B symbolic
never colours it and
.B colour
always does.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH ACCESS
Reading the daemon's status requires membership of the
.B timeshift
group. Nothing grants it automatically: it also exposes the machine's disk
layout and every snapshot path, so it is an administrator's decision. The
applet's menu offers to arrange it, or:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo gpasswd \-a "$USER" timeshift
.fi
.RE
.PP
The change takes effect at the next login. To take it away again use the
applet's "Remove status access" item, or:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo gpasswd \-d "$USER" timeshift
.fi
.RE
.SH REQUIREMENTS
A tray icon needs a host implementing the StatusNotifierItem protocol. KDE
Plasma, Xfce, MATE and Cinnamon provide one natively; GNOME Shell needs an
appindicator extension:
.PP
.RS
.nf
gnome\-extensions enable ubuntu\-appindicators@ubuntu.com
.fi
.RE
.PP
With no such host the applet keeps running and says so once in the journal; the
icon appears if a host arrives later.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/autostart/timeshift\-tray.desktop
Autostart entry. Disable it through the desktop's own startup\-applications
settings rather than by editing this file.
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/timeshift\-tray/
The three fixed\-argument wrappers run through
.BR pkexec (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timeshift (1),
.BR pkexec (1)
.SH AUTHOR
makeafide <willsmit4433@gmail.com>
